-- title:
--   §1–§6, pp. 3–20 — the model ℱ, 𝒦_inf (2), the sets 𝒞_{μ,γ} (6), empirical distributions, the bound U of (15) and runs of Algorithm 3
-- statement:
--   This file fixes the objects of the analysis of the empirical KL-UCB algorithm of Cappé, Garivier, Maillard, Munos and Stoltz for bounded, finitely supported rewards. Throughout, measures live on $\mathbb R$ with its Borel $\sigma$-algebra, $\mathrm{KL}(\nu,\nu')$ is the Kullback–Leibler divergence (with values in $[0,+\infty]$), and $\mathrm E(\nu) = \int x\,d\nu(x)$ is the expectation of $\nu$.
--
--   1. **The model $\mathcal F$** (§5, p. 14). $\nu\in\mathcal F$ when $\nu$ is a probability measure carried by a finite subset of $[0,1]$, i.e. a finitely supported probability distribution over $\mathcal S = [0,1]$.
--   2. **The minimal divergence (2)** (p. 3), for the model $\mathcal D = \mathcal F$:
--   $$\mathcal K_{\inf}(\nu,\mu) = \inf\bigl\{\mathrm{KL}(\nu,\nu') : \nu'\in\mathcal F \text{ and } \mathrm E(\nu') > \mu\bigr\}\in[0,+\infty],$$
--   with $\inf\emptyset = +\infty$.
--   3. **The sets (6)** (p. 9), for $\mathcal D = \mathcal F$ and $\Pi_{\mathcal D}$ the identity: $\nu\in\mathcal C_{\mu,\gamma}$ when there is $\nu'\in\mathcal F$ with $\mathrm E(\nu') > \mu$ and $\mathrm{KL}(\nu,\nu')\le\gamma$.
--   4. **Empirical distributions** (§2.2, p. 7). For a sequence $x_1, x_2, \dots$ and $n\ge 1$, $\hat\nu_n = \frac1n\sum_{k=1}^n \delta_{x_k}$, with finite support $\mathrm{Supp}(\hat\nu_n) = \{x_1,\dots,x_n\}$.
--   5. **The upper confidence bound (15)** (p. 19). For a finite set $S\subset\mathbb R$, a measure $\hat\nu$ and $\varepsilon\in\mathbb R$,
--   $$U(\hat\nu,\varepsilon) = \sup\bigl\{\mathrm E(\nu') : \nu'\in\mathfrak M_1(S\cup\{1\}) \text{ and } \mathrm{KL}(\hat\nu,\nu')\le\varepsilon\bigr\},$$
--   where $\mathfrak M_1(A)$ is the set of probability measures carried by $A$; it is applied with $S = \mathrm{Supp}(\hat\nu)$.
--   6. **The index of Algorithm 3** (p. 15). A bandit with $K$ arms is described by its reward stacks: $X_{a,k}$ is the reward of the $(k+1)$-st pull of arm $a$. Arm $A_t$ is pulled at round $t\ge1$, $N_a(t) = \sum_{s=1}^t \mathbb I\{A_s = a\}$, and $\hat\nu_a(t) = \hat\nu_{a,N_a(t)}$ is the empirical distribution of the first $N_a(t)$ rewards of arm $a$ (the identity of p. 7). The index of arm $a$ after round $t$, with exploration function $f$, is
--   $$U_a(t) = \sup\Bigl\{\mathrm E(\nu) : \nu\in\mathfrak M_1\bigl(\mathrm{Supp}(\hat\nu_a(t))\cup\{1\}\bigr) \text{ and } \mathrm{KL}(\hat\nu_a(t),\nu)\le \frac{f(t)}{N_a(t)}\Bigr\}.$$
--   7. **Runs.** A sequence of choices $(A_t)_{t\ge1}$ is a run of an index policy with indices $U$ when rounds $1,\dots,K$ pull every arm once (the arm pulled at round $t+1\le K$ has not been pulled before) and, for every $t\ge K$, the arm pulled at round $t+1$ maximizes $U_\cdot(t)$, ties broken by any rule. A run of Algorithm 3 is a run with the indices of item 6.
--   8. **The exploration function of Theorem 2**: $f(t) = \log t + \log\log t$, used for $t\ge 2$.
--   9. **Non-anticipation** (§2, p. 5). The reward observed at round $s$ is $Y_s = X_{A_s, N_{A_s}(s-1)}$; the history after round $t$ is the $\sigma$-algebra generated by $A_1, Y_1,\dots,A_t,Y_t$; a strategy is non-anticipating when every $A_{t+1}$ is measurable with respect to the history after round $t$.
--
--   These are the objects on which Theorem 2, its proof skeleton (5)–(10) and Proposition 1 are stated.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Arms are `Fin K` (the paper's arm $a$ is index $a-1$) and stack indices start at $0$. KL is Mathlib's `InformationTheory.klDiv`, valued in $[0,+\infty]$, always with the empirical measure as first argument; $\mathcal K_{\inf}$ is kept in $[0,+\infty]$. "$\nu'\in\mathfrak M_1(A)$" is written as "$\nu'$ is a probability measure with $\nu'(A^c) = 0$". The supremum in 5 is a real `sSup`: for finite $S$ its set is bounded above by $\max(S\cup\{1\})$, and when $\hat\nu$ is the empirical distribution of $n\ge1$ values it contains $\mathrm E(\hat\nu)$ (take $\nu' = \hat\nu$), so it is not a junk value. The index is only used for $t\ge K$, where $N_a(t)\ge1$. $f(0)$ and $f(1)$ are Lean's junk values and are never evaluated; $f(2)\approx 0.327>0$ and $f$ is increasing on $t\ge 2$. The run predicate is anytime (no horizon $T$).
-- source:
--   Cappé, Garivier, Maillard, Munos, Stoltz, Kullback–Leibler upper confidence bounds for optimal sequential allocation, arXiv:1210.1136v4, p. 3 (2), pp. 5–7 (§2), p. 7 (Algorithm 1), p. 9 (6), p. 14 (§5, model ℱ), p. 15 (Algorithm 3, Theorem 2), p. 19 (15)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_RegretBandits_Stochastic_model
import Definitions.Def_CappeKLUCB_ExpFam_Setting

namespace CappeKLUCB.Empirical

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory ImprovedLinBandits.UCBDelta RegretBandits.Stochastic
open scoped ENNReal

/-- The expectation `E(ν) = ∫ x dν(x)` of a measure `ν` on `ℝ` (Cappé et al., arXiv:1210.1136v4,
§2.1, p. 5). Only applied to probability measures carried by a finite subset of `[0, 1]` or by
`[0, 1]`, for which the integral is a genuine (finite) integral. -/
noncomputable def mean (ν : Measure ℝ) : ℝ :=
  ∫ x, x ∂ν

/-- `ν ∈ ℱ` (§5, p. 14): `ν` is a probability measure on `ℝ` carried by a finite subset of
`𝒮 = [0, 1]`, i.e. a finitely supported probability distribution over `[0, 1]`. -/
def IsFinSupp01 (ν : Measure ℝ) : Prop :=
  IsProbabilityMeasure ν ∧ ∃ S : Finset ℝ, (↑S : Set ℝ) ⊆ Set.Icc 0 1 ∧ ν (↑S)ᶜ = 0

/-- `𝒦_inf(ν, μ) = inf {KL(ν, ν′) : ν′ ∈ ℱ and E(ν′) > μ}`, display (2) of p. 3 with the model
`𝒟 = ℱ` of §5. Valued in `ℝ≥0∞`; the infimum of the empty family is `+∞`. The Kullback–Leibler
divergence is Mathlib's `InformationTheory.klDiv`, with `ν` as its first argument. -/
noncomputable def Kinf (ν : Measure ℝ) (μ : ℝ) : ℝ≥0∞ :=
  ⨅ (ν' : Measure ℝ) (_ : IsFinSupp01 ν') (_ : μ < mean ν'), InformationTheory.klDiv ν ν'

/-- `ν ∈ 𝒞_{μ,γ}`, display (6) of p. 9 with `𝒟 = ℱ` and `Π_𝒟` the identity (§5, p. 14):
there is `ν′ ∈ ℱ` with `E(ν′) > μ` and `KL(ν, ν′) ≤ γ`. -/
def InC (μ γ : ℝ) (ν : Measure ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∃ ν' : Measure ℝ, IsFinSupp01 ν' ∧ μ < mean ν' ∧
    InformationTheory.klDiv ν ν' ≤ ENNReal.ofReal γ

/-- The empirical distribution `ν̂_n = n⁻¹ ∑_{k=1}^n δ_{x_k}` of the first `n` values of a sequence
(§2.2, p. 7; §6.2, p. 19). Index `k` of the sequence is the paper's `k + 1`. At `n = 0` this is the
zero measure; it is never used there. -/
noncomputable def empMeasure (x : ℕ → ℝ) (n : ℕ) : Measure ℝ :=
  (n : ℝ≥0∞)⁻¹ • ∑ k ∈ Finset.range n, Measure.dirac (x k)

/-- `Supp(ν̂_n)`, the finite support `{x_1, …, x_n}` of the empirical distribution of the first `n`
values of a sequence. -/
noncomputable def empSupp (x : ℕ → ℝ) (n : ℕ) : Finset ℝ :=
  (Finset.range n).image x

/-- The upper confidence bound (15) of p. 19,
`U(ν̂, ε) = sup {E(ν′) : ν′ ∈ 𝔐₁(S ∪ {1}) and KL(ν̂, ν′) ≤ ε}`, with `S = Supp(ν̂)`:
the supremum of the expectations of the probability measures `ν′` carried by `S ∪ {1}` whose
Kullback–Leibler divergence from `ν̂` is at most `ε`. When `S ⊆ ℝ` is finite the set is bounded
above by `max (S ∪ {1})`, and when `ν̂` is a probability measure carried by `S` it contains `E(ν̂)`
(take `ν′ = ν̂`), so the real supremum is a genuine supremum. -/
noncomputable def elUpper (S : Finset ℝ) (νh : Measure ℝ) (ε : ℝ) : ℝ :=
  sSup {m : ℝ | ∃ ν' : Measure ℝ, IsProbabilityMeasure ν' ∧ ν' (↑(insert 1 S) : Set ℝ)ᶜ = 0 ∧
    InformationTheory.klDiv νh ν' ≤ ENNReal.ofReal ε ∧ m = mean ν'}

/-- The index `U_a(t)` of Algorithm 3 (p. 15) with exploration function `f`:
`U_a(t) = sup {E(ν) : ν ∈ 𝔐₁(Supp(ν̂_a(t)) ∪ {1}) and KL(ν̂_a(t), ν) ≤ f(t)/N_a(t)}`, written through
the stack of rewards: `ν̂_a(t) = ν̂_{a, N_a(t)}` is the empirical distribution of the first `N_a(t)`
rewards `X a 0 ω, …, X a (N_a(t) - 1) ω` of arm `a` (the identity of p. 7). Only used for `t ≥ K`,
where `N_a(t) ≥ 1`. -/
noncomputable def index (f : ℕ → ℝ) {Ω : Type*} {K : ℕ} (X : Fin K → ℕ → Ω → ℝ)
    (I : ℕ → Ω → Fin K) (a : Fin K) (t : ℕ) (ω : Ω) : ℝ :=
  elUpper (empSupp (fun k => X a k ω) (pullCount I a t ω))
    (empMeasure (fun k => X a k ω) (pullCount I a t ω)) (f t / (pullCount I a t ω : ℝ))

/-- A run of the empirical KL-UCB algorithm (Algorithm 3, p. 15) with exploration function `f`. -/
def IsEmpKLUCBRun (f : ℕ → ℝ) {Ω : Type*} {K : ℕ} (X : Fin K → ℕ → Ω → ℝ)
    (I : ℕ → Ω → Fin K) : Prop :=
  CappeKLUCB.ExpFam.IsIndexRun (index f X I) I

/-- The exploration function of Theorem 2 (p. 15), `f(t) = log(t) + log(log(t))` for `t ≥ 2`.
Algorithm 3 evaluates it only at rounds `t ≥ K ≥ 2`, where it is positive and increasing
(`f(2) = log 2 + log log 2 ≈ 0.327`); its values at `t = 0, 1` are Lean's junk values and are
never used. -/
noncomputable def f₂ (t : ℕ) : ℝ :=
  Real.log t + Real.log (Real.log t)

end CappeKLUCB.Empirical


